Move match exhaustiveness check into algebra/

Add `algebra/match_check.rs` with `check_rule_matches`, a per-rule pass
over the AST and `Signature` that emits `MatchConflictingEnum` and
`MatchNotExhaustive`. For each `match` statement in the rule's body it
resolves each case's pattern to a constructor via `Scopes::lookup`,
groups the constructors by their enum and reports a conflict when more
than one enum is involved or a missing constructor when the enum's
full ctor list is not covered.

Invoke it from `build_structures` after the populate/close fixed point
converges for each rule, so match diagnostics participate in the same
per-rule error vector as type conflicts and arg-count mismatches.
`build_structures` already bails on the first rule with errors, so a
match error in an earlier rule short-circuits the same way a type
conflict would, and a later rule's diagnostics never mask an earlier
rule's broken match.

Drop the corresponding eqlog-side machinery from eqlog.eql:
case_pattern_is_*, pattern_ctor_arg_*, match_case_pattern_ctor,
cases_contain_ctor, match_stmt_contains_ctor_of_enum,
cases_determined_enum, match_term_type_if_cases_determine_enum,
match_stmt_should_contain_ctor and match_stmt_contains_ctor, together
with their defining rules. case_pattern_app_should_be_constructor
stays since it feeds the generic should_be_symbol lookup. Regenerate
prebuilt/eqlog.rs. Drop iter_match_conflicting_enum and
iter_match_stmt_contains_ctor_of_enum from semantics::check_eqlog,
the only Rust consumers of the removed eqlog state.

Add Signature::enum_decl_for_type to invert type_for_enum_decl, used
by the new pass to map a constructor's codomain back to its enum
declaration.
